{"name":"Sage Leaves","hex":"#7b8b5d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sage","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Wise.","Grave; serious; solemn."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A very wise person or spiritual teacher; someone of gravity and wisdom, especially, a teacher venerable for years, and of sound judgment and prudence; a grave or stoic philosopher."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sage"},{"term":"leaves","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of leaf","from leave:\nplural of leave \nplural of leave"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of leave"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/leaves"}],"entries":[{"title":"Salvia officinalis","blocks":[{"text":"Salvia officinalis, common sage or sage, is a perennial, evergreen subshrub, with woody stems, grayish leaves, and blue to purplish flowers. It is a member of the mint family (Lamiaceae) and native to the Mediterranean region, though it has been naturalized in many places throughout the world. It has a long history of culinary use, and in modern times it has been used as an ornamental garden plant. The common name \"sage\" is also used for closely related species and cultivars."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Salvia_officinalis0.jpg/330px-Salvia_officinalis0.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salvia_officinalis","wikidata":"Q1111359"},{"title":"Sage (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Sage is a grey-green resembling that of dried sage leaves. As a quaternary color, it is an equal mix of the tertiary colors citron and slate. The hex RGB color value of the adjacent sage swatch is BCB88A. For decades, some military flight jackets were made in sage green."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Sage_iPhone_17.jpg/330px-Sage_iPhone_17.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sage_(color)","wikidata":"Q7399114"},{"title":"Sage (Marvel Comics)","blocks":[{"text":"Sage is a character appearing in American comic books published by Marvel Comics. She has most often been associated with the X-Men and the Hellfire Club, whom she spied upon under the alias Tessa for Charles Xavier."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sage_(Marvel_Comics)","wikidata":"Q2258275"},{"title":"Leaf","blocks":[{"text":"A leaf is a principal appendage of the stem of a vascular plant, usually borne laterally above ground and specialized for photosynthesis. Leaves are collectively called foliage, as in \"autumn foliage\", while the leaves, stem, flower, and fruit collectively form the shoot system. In most leaves, the primary photosynthetic tissue is the palisade mesophyll and is located on the upper side of the blade or lamina of the leaf, but in some species, including the mature foliage of Eucalyptus, palisade mesophyll is present on both sides and the leaves are said to be isobilateral. The leaf is an integral part of the stem system, and most leaves are flattened and have distinct upper (adaxial) and lower (abaxial) surfaces that differ in color, hairiness, the number of stomata, the amount and structure of epicuticular wax, and other features. Leaves are mostly green in color due to the presence of a compound called chlorophyll, which is essential for photosynthesis as it absorbs light energy from the Sun. A leaf with lighter-colored or white patches or edges is called a variegated leaf."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Lisc_lipy.jpg/330px-Lisc_lipy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leaf","wikidata":"Q33971"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Salvia_officinalis_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-126.jpg/330px-Salvia_officinalis_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-126.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Salvia_officinalis_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-126.jpg/330px-Salvia_officinalis_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-126.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Painting from Koehler's Medicinal Plants (1887)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKoeh-126.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Salvia_officinalis_Berggarten.JPG/250px-Salvia_officinalis_Berggarten.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Salvia_officinalis_Berggarten.JPG/330px-Salvia_officinalis_Berggarten.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Salvia_officinalis_Berggarten.JPG/250px-Salvia_officinalis_Berggarten.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASalvia_officinalis_Berggarten.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Salvia_officinalis_%27Icterina%27.jpg/250px-Salvia_officinalis_%27Icterina%27.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Salvia_officinalis_%27Icterina%27.jpg/500px-Salvia_officinalis_%27Icterina%27.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Salvia_officinalis_%27Icterina%27.jpg/250px-Salvia_officinalis_%27Icterina%27.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASalvia_officinalis_'Icterina'.jpg"},